DAY 8 - PART A: THEORY
YAML Basics and the Declarative Model
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHY YAML?
------------
Last week you used "k run" (an IMPERATIVE command: "do this now").
Real teams describe objects in YAML files, store them in Git, and
apply them. The file IS the desired state. This is DECLARATIVE.

  Imperative:   k run web --image=nginx
  Declarative:  k apply -f web.yaml   (file says what should exist)

Declarative wins because files are repeatable, reviewable, and
version-controlled. "k apply" can be run 100 times safely.


2. YAML IN 5 RULES
------------------
1) key: value pairs              name: web
2) Indentation = nesting. SPACES only, never tabs. Use 2 spaces.
3) A list item starts with "- "
       containers:
       - name: web
         image: nginx
4) Strings usually need no quotes. Quote things that look like other
   types: "yes", "no", "on", "1.20", "08".
5) Multiple documents in one file are separated by a line with ---

Most YAML errors are indentation errors. Check with:
   k apply -f file.yaml --dry-run=client


3. THE FOUR REQUIRED TOP-LEVEL FIELDS
-------------------------------------
Every Kubernetes object has:

  apiVersion:  which API group/version  (v1, apps/v1, batch/v1 ...)
  kind:        type of object           (Pod, Deployment, Service ...)
  metadata:    name, namespace, labels, annotations
  spec:        what you want (desired state)

(status is added by Kubernetes; you never write it.)

Example:

  apiVersion: v1
  kind: Pod
  metadata:
    name: web
    labels:
      app: web
  spec:
    containers:
    - name: web
      image: nginx:1.27
      ports:
      - containerPort: 80


4. HOW TO KNOW WHICH apiVersion?
--------------------------------
  k api-resources            shows NAME, SHORTNAMES, APIVERSION, KIND
  k explain pod              docs for the object
  k explain pod.spec.containers.ports    drill into any field
  k explain deployment --recursive | less   all fields

You do not need to memorise YAML. You need to know how to generate it
and how to look up fields.


5. GENERATE YAML INSTEAD OF TYPING IT FROM ZERO
-----------------------------------------------
  k run web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > pod.yaml
  k create deployment web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > deploy.yaml

--dry-run=client means "build the object locally, do not send it".
-o yaml prints it. This trick is used daily (and in the CKA exam).


6. create vs apply vs replace vs delete
---------------------------------------
  k create -f x.yaml    create; FAILS if it already exists
  k apply -f x.yaml     create OR update to match the file (use this)
  k replace -f x.yaml   replace the whole object
  k delete -f x.yaml    delete what the file describes
  k diff -f x.yaml      show what apply would change

Some pod fields cannot be changed after creation (for example the
container ports or most of spec). Pods are mostly immutable: you delete
and recreate. Deployments handle this for you (Day 11).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- apiVersion, kind, metadata, spec.
- Spaces only, 2-space indentation, lists start with "- ".
- Generate with --dry-run=client -o yaml; look up with k explain.
- Use k apply for everything.
